  "upgrade_suggestion": "Your original 1980 Howie Weinberg cut (MASTERDISK HW) is already one of the best-sounding pressings of the album. A highly regarded alternative cut is the original Canadian pressing mastered by Bob Ludwig (RL MASTERDISK in runout), prized for its punchier bass and drums. For high-resolution digital playback on your Sony UBP-X700, look for the 2011 high-res 24-bit/96kHz digital remaster or the 1989 MFSL Gold CD (UDCD 505).",
  "commentary": "Released on January 1, 1980 by Mercury Records, *Permanent Waves* is the seventh studio album by Canadian rock band Rush. It represents a major stylistic turning point for the band, shifting from the extended fantasy/sci-fi suites of *A Farewell to Kings* and *Hemispheres* toward tighter, more accessible songwriting that incorporated elements of new wave and reggae. Co-produced by the band and Terry Brown, the album was recorded at Le Studio in Morin-Heights, Quebec—a picturesque studio that would become the band's creative home throughout the 1980s. The record yielded some of the band's most enduring radio hits, including \"The Spirit of Radio\" and \"Freewill,\" alongside progressive showpieces like \"Jacob's Ladder\" and the multi-part epic \"Natural Science.\"\n\nThe album's production, engineered by Paul Northfield, is highly celebrated by audiophiles for its punchy dynamics, transparent high-end, and powerful bass response. The mixing was completed at Trident Studios in London. While the custom inner sleeve lyric sheet credits mastering to Ray Staff at Trident, the original US Mercury pressings were actually mastered stateside at Masterdisk in New York.\n\n**About this 1980 PRC Richmond pressing (LP, Stereo):** Mercury Records catalog `SRM-1-4001` (and international catalog number `9110.143` on back cover/labels). This copy was pressed at the PRC Recording Company's plant in Richmond, Indiana, identified by the pressing plant code `72` printed on the labels and the `PRC-R` etching in the deadwax.\n\nThe lacquers for this pressing were cut by mastering legend **Howie Weinberg at Masterdisk** (evident from the `MASTERDISK HW` inscriptions). Weinberg’s cut of *Permanent Waves* is widely considered by collectors to be the definitive sounding version of the album on vinyl, praised for its clarity, soundstage width, and bass definition. \n\nYour copy's Side A runout contains the matrix `SRM-1-4001-A-PRC-R-1-4` (inscribed on some copies as `SRM-14001-A-PRC-R-1-4`) alongside the stamped `MASTERDISK HW` credits, indicating a master metalwork transfer plated for PRC Richmond's pressing lines.\n",
